  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTuesday will be the coolest day of the week as we remain in a much \nmore comfortable post-frontal air mass and under the influence of a \n1020 mb surface high to our north. While the bulk of the forcing for \nascent will remain well to our northeast over Manitoba and Ontario, \ndecreasing 500 mb heights that actually fall below the median value \nrelative to climatology will bring us some much-desired and \nseasonably cool conditions across SE Wyoming and the Nebraska \nPanhandle. Strengthening ESE gradient flow will be in place thanks \nto an inverted ridge being banking up against the higher terrain \nwill also bring us some breezy conditions. Despite it being another \ndry day, we can expect much more comfortable temperatures with highs \nranging from the mid to upper 70s east of a Laramie to Douglas line, \nwith low 80s expected further west where fewer clouds are expected. \nLong-range ensemble guidance is suggesting wind gusts ranging from \n25-30 mph mainly east of the Laramie Range, however 25th percentile \nRH values are around 25-35%, so Red Flag conditions are unlikely at \nthis time, but will need to be monitored depending on if the \nmagnitude of low-level upslope flow decreases. Enjoy the cooler \nweather while it lasts...\n\nThe warm up begins on Wednesday as a mid-level ridge builds over the \nDesert Southwest, placing us in a northwesterly flow regime, which \nis not exactly ideal in terms of monsoonal moisture transport from \nthe southwest. Highs on Wednesday will generally be in the upper 70s \nto low 80s with calmer winds compared to the previous day. The trend \ncontinues into Thursday as the ridge axis shifts north and east \ncloser to the Rocky Mountain West, resulting in high temperatures \napproaching 90 degrees. Ending the work week and heading into the \nweekend, ensemble guidance has the ridge continuing to strengthen \nand shift east over the Great Basin, returning the possibility of \nseeing near record-high temperatures in the mid 90s to near 100 east \nof the I-25 corridor, and upper 80s to low 90s to the west. Dry \nconditions remain in place underneath the ridge until at least the \nweekend with no meaningful precipitation chances as PWAT remains \nbetween the 10th to 20th percentiles.",
